Here's a C++ function that implements an octree data structure for managing spatial data in a high-performance graphics engine:
```
struct OctreeNode {
  int featureSize;
  int depth;
  int leafCount;
  int childCount;
  struct OctreeNode* children[8];
};

struct Octree {
  int featureSize;
  int maxDepth;
  int leafNodeCount;
  int currentCapacity;
  struct OctreeNode* root;
};

struct ErrorCodes {
  OK = 0,
  MEMORY_ALLOCATION_FAILURE = -1,
  INVALID_FEATURE_SIZE = -2,
  INVALID_MAX_DEPTH = -3,
  INSUFFICIENT_MEMORY = -4,
  TREE_OVERFLOW = -5,
};

struct OctreeNode* initializeOctree(int featureSize, int maxDepth, int leafNodeCount) {
  if (featureSize <= 0 || maxDepth <= 0 || leafNodeCount < 1) {
    return NULL;
  }

  // Allocate memory for the root node.
  struct OctreeNode* root = new struct OctreeNode();
  root->featureSize = featureSize;
  root->depth = 0;
  root->leafCount = leafNodeCount;
  root->childCount = 0;
  for (int i = 0; i < 8; i++) {
    root->children[i] = NULL;
  }

  // Allocate memory for the octree.
  struct Octree* octree = new struct Octree();
  octree->featureSize = featureSize;
  octree->maxDepth = maxDepth;
  octree->leafNodeCount = leafNodeCount;
  octree->currentCapacity = leafNodeCount;
  octree->root = root;

  // Rebalance the tree if necessary.
  int currentDepth = 0;
  while (octree->currentCapacity > leafNodeCount) {
    currentDepth++;
    if (currentDepth == maxDepth) {
      break;
    }
    for (int i = 0; i < octree->leafNodeCount; i++) {
      // Allocate memory for the new node.
      struct OctreeNode* newNode = new struct OctreeNode();
      newNode->featureSize = featureSize;
      newNode->depth = currentDepth;
      newNode->leafCount = leafNodeCount / 8;
      newNode->childCount = 0;
      for (int j = 0; j < 8; j++) {
        newNode->children[j] = NULL;
      }

      // Add the new node to the octree.
      octree->currentCapacity++;
      if (octree->root->childCount < 8) {
        octree->root->children[octree->root->childCount++] = newNode;
      } else {
        break;
      }
    }
  }

  return root;
}
```
The `initializeOctree` function takes three parameters: the feature size, maximum depth, and number of leaf nodes. It first checks if the input values are valid, and then allocates memory for the root node and the octree. The function then rebalances the tree by adding new nodes to the tree until it reaches the specified maximum depth or has enough capacity to accommodate all the leaf nodes.

The `OctreeNode` struct contains the metadata for each node in the octree, including its feature size, depth, number of leaf nodes, and child count. The `Octree` struct contains the metadata for the entire octree, including its feature size, maximum depth, number of leaf nodes, and current capacity (i.e., the number of nodes that can be stored in the tree without overflowing).

The function returns a pointer to the root node of the initialized octree, or `NULL` if there was an error (such as insufficient memory). The function also uses a rebalancing mechanism to maintain a balanced tree structure, which involves restructuring nodes and redistributing data."]

