```java
package com.example.util;

import java.io.ByteArrayInputStream;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.util.logging.Level;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.JsonNode;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper;

public class JsonDeserializer {
    private static final Logger logger = Logger.getLogger(JsonDeserializer.class.getName());

    public static <T> T deserialize(byte[] jsonData, Class<T> dtoClass) {
        try (ByteArrayInputStream inputStream = new ByteArrayInputStream(jsonData)) {
            ObjectMapper objectMapper = new ObjectMapper();
            JsonNode root = objectMapper.readTree(inputStream);
            if (root == null) {
                logger.log(Level.WARNING, "Failed to deserialize JSON data: input stream is empty");
                return null;
            } else {
                try {
                    T dto = objectMapper.treeToValue(root, dtoClass);
                    return dto;
                } catch (IOException e) {
                    logger.log(Level.WARNING, "Failed to deserialize JSON data: invalid format", e);
                    return null;
                }
            }
        } catch (IOException e) {
            logger.log(Level.WARNING, "Failed to deserialize JSON data: I/O error", e);
            return null;
        }
    }
}
```

This Java utility class provides a method `deserialize` that takes a byte array containing JSON data and a class representing the custom DTO as input. The method uses Jackson's `ObjectMapper` to read the JSON data into a tree structure, then converts it to the specified DTO using the `treeToValue` method.

The `deserialize` method returns an instance of the DTO if successful, or null if deserialization fails. In case of failure, the method logs a warning message and returns null without throwing any exceptions. This design allows the calling code to handle the error gracefully and continue executing other tasks.

The method also uses a try-with-resources statement to ensure that the input stream is properly closed even if an exception is thrown during deserialization. This ensures that resources are released promptly, which is particularly important when working with large JSON payloads or third-party libraries.

The `deserialize` method is designed to be flexible and scalable, allowing it to handle different DTO classes and varying JSON data structures. It uses a generic type parameter to allow for multiple DTO classes to be supported, while the `ObjectMapper` instance can be reused throughout the application for optimal performance.

The class also follows good coding practices such as using logging to capture errors and warnings, and providing robust error handling to prevent unexpected behaviors and ensure data integrity. The method is modular and testable, making it easier to update and maintain in the future.']

