    record **new_records = (record **)calloc(ht->capacity, sizeof(record *));
    for (int i = 0; i < ht->size; i++) {
        new_records[i] = ht->records[i];
    }

    // Free the old records array and replace it with the new one
    free(ht->records);
    ht->records = new_records;
}

void resize_hash_table() {
    // Increase the capacity of the hash table by a factor of 2
    ht->capacity = 2 * ht->capacity;

    // Reallocate the records array with the new capacity
    record **new_records = (record **)calloc(ht->capacity, sizeof(record *));
    for (int i = 0; i < ht->size; i++) {
        new_records[i] = ht->records[i];
    }

    // Free the old records array and replace it with the new one
    free(ht->records);
    ht->records = new_records;
}
```
This code implements a caching mechanism for managing up to 2000 unique records, each including an integer value, a string pointer, and event information. The cache uses an LRU eviction policy with custom hash table resizing and prime-number-based rehashing to handle collisions and accommodate growth. The hash table is lock-free to improve performance.

The code also defines monitoring functions for tracking the records in the cache and their access patterns. These functions use a circularly linked list of monitor nodes to keep track of the records and their usage.

Finally, this code provides resizing functions for both the cache and the hash table to handle overflows and ensure that they can accommodate growing data sets.']

