### Solution

Here's a solution in C++ for the LRU Cache problem. This implementation uses a hash table to store the keys and values of the cache, and maintains an ordered list of recently accessed items. When the cache is full, the least recently used item is evicted from the cache.

```cpp
class LRUCache {
    private:
        // Maximum number of items in the cache
        int capacity;

        // Hash table to store keys and values
        std::unordered_map<std::string, std::pair<void*, size_t>> hash;

        // Ordered list to keep track of recently accessed items
        std::list<std::pair<std::string, void*>> order;
    public:
        LRUCache(int capacity) {
            this->capacity = capacity;
        }

        void insert(std::string key, void* value) {
            // Check if the cache is full
            if (hash.size() == capacity) {
                // Evict the least recently used item
                evict();
            }

            // Insert the new item into the hash table and ordered list
            hash[key] = std::make_pair(value, 0);
            order.push_back({ key, value });
        }

        void retrieve(std::string key) {
            // Check if the key exists in the cache
            auto it = hash.find(key);
            if (it == hash.end()) {
                return;
            }

            // Increase the frequency of the item
            std::pair<void*, size_t>& pair = it->second;
            order.erase(std::remove(order.begin(), order.end(), key), order.end());
            order.push_back({ key, pair.first });
            pair.second++;
        }

        void evict() {
            // Remove the least recently used item from the cache
            std::pair<std::string, void*> pair = order.front();
            hash.erase(pair.first);
            order.pop_front();
        }
};
```

Time complexity: O(1) for insert and retrieve operations since they have a constant time complexity. The evict operation has a worst-case time complexity of O(capacity), but the average time complexity is O(1) since it is called when the cache is full.

Space complexity: O(capacity) for the hash table to store the keys and values of the cache, and O(capacity) for the ordered list to keep track of recently accessed items.

### Solution in Java

Here's a solution in Java for the LRU Cache problem. This implementation uses a LinkedHashMap to store the keys and values of the cache, which keeps the most recently used items at the beginning of the map. When the cache is full, the least recently used item is evicted from the cache.

```java
import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

public class LRUCache {
    private static final int DEFAULT_CAPACITY = 10;

    private Map<String, Integer> cache;
    private List<Integer> order;

    public LRUCache() {
        this(DEFAULT_CAPACITY);
    }

    public LRUCache(int capacity) {
        cache = new LinkedHashMap<>();
        order = new ArrayList<>();
    }

    public void insert(String key, int value) {
        if (cache.size() == capacity()) {
            evict();
        }

        // Add the new item to the hash table and ordered list
        cache.put(key, value);
        order.add(value);
    }

    public void retrieve(String key) {
        // Check if the key exists in the cache
        Integer value = cache.get(key);
        if (value == null) {
            return;
        }

        // Increase the frequency of the item
        order.remove(new Integer(value));
        order.add(0, value);
    }

    public void evict() {
        // Remove the least recently used item from the cache
        Integer last = order.get(order.size() - 1);
        cache.remove(last);
        order.remove(last);
    }

    private int capacity() {
        return cache.size();
    }
}
```

Time complexity: O(1) for insert and retrieve operations since they have a constant time complexity. The evict operation has a worst-case time complexity of O(capacity), but the average time complexity is O(1) since it is called when the cache is full.

Space complexity: O(capacity) for the hash table to store the keys and values of the cache, and O(capacity) for the ordered list to keep track of recently accessed items.
