## [SOLUTION] 3.1

The `dynamic_array` struct is a custom data structure that represents an array with dynamic size. It has the following fields:

* `data`: a pointer to the array of elements
* `capacity`: the current capacity of the array (i.e., the maximum number of elements it can hold)
* `size`: the current size of the array (i.e., the number of elements it contains)

The `dynamic_array` struct provides several functions to manipulate its data:

* `initialize()`: initializes a new dynamic array with a given capacity
* `resize()`: resizes the dynamic array to a given size
* `push_back()`: adds an element to the end of the dynamic array
* `pop_back()`: removes the last element from the dynamic array
* `at()`: returns a reference to the element at a given position in the dynamic array
* `operator[]` (subscript operator): returns a reference to the element at a given position in the dynamic array (same as `at()`)

Here is an example of how you can use these functions to implement a stack using a dynamic array:
```cpp
int main() {
    // Create a new dynamic array with capacity 10
    dynamic_array<int> stack(10);

    // Push some elements onto the stack
    for (int i = 0; i < 5; ++i) {
        stack.push_back(i * 2);
    }

    // Pop an element from the stack
    int lastElement = stack.pop_back();
    std::cout << "Last element: " << lastElement << std::endl;

    // Get the top of the stack
    int topOfStack = stack[0];
    std::cout << "Top of stack: " << topOfStack << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```
This example shows how to create a new dynamic array with capacity 10, push some elements onto the stack, pop an element from the stack, and get the top of the stack.

