## [SOLUTION] 3.2

Here is an example implementation of the `dynamic_array` struct:
```cpp
template<typename T>
struct dynamic_array {
    // Pointer to the array of elements
    T* data;

    // Capacity of the array (maximum number of elements it can hold)
    size_t capacity;

    // Current size of the array (number of elements it contains)
    size_t size;

    // Initializes a new dynamic array with a given capacity
    explicit dynamic_array(size_t capacity) : data(new T[capacity]), capacity(capacity), size(0) {}

    // Resizes the dynamic array to a given size
    void resize(size_t newSize) {
        if (newSize > capacity) {
            // If the new size is greater than the current capacity, we need to reallocate more space
            T* newData = new T[newSize];
            for (size_t i = 0; i < size; ++i) {
                newData[i] = data[i];
            }
            delete[] data;
            data = newData;
            capacity = newSize;
        } else if (newSize < size) {
            // If the new size is smaller than the current size, we need to remove some elements from the array
            for (size_t i = newSize; i < size; ++i) {
                data[i] = T();
            }
        }
        size = newSize;
    }

    // Adds an element to the end of the dynamic array
    void push_back(const T& element) {
        if (size == capacity) {
            resize(capacity * 2);
        }
        data[size] = element;
        ++size;
    }

    // Removes the last element from the dynamic array
    void pop_back() {
        --size;
    }

    // Returns a reference to the element at a given position in the dynamic array
    T& operator[](size_t index) {
        return data[index];
    }

    // Returns a reference to the element at a given position in the dynamic array (same as `operator[]`)
    const T& at(size_t index) const {
        return data[index];
    }
};
```
This implementation defines a `dynamic_array` struct that takes a template parameter `T` for the type of elements it will hold. It has three fields:

* `data`: a pointer to the array of elements
* `capacity`: the current capacity of the array (i.e., the maximum number of elements it can hold)
* `size`: the current size of the array (i.e., the number of elements it contains)

The struct provides several functions to manipulate its data:

* `initialize()`: initializes a new dynamic array with a given capacity
* `resize()`: resizes the dynamic array to a given size
* `push_back()`: adds an element to the end of the dynamic array
* `pop_back()`: removes the last element from the dynamic array
* `at()`: returns a reference to the element at a given position in the dynamic array
* `operator[]` (subscript operator): returns a reference to the element at a given position in the dynamic array (same as `at()`)

The `initialize()` function takes a capacity argument and initializes the dynamic array with that capacity. The `resize()` function takes a new size argument and resizes the dynamic array to have that many elements. The `push_back()` function adds an element to the end of the dynamic array, while the `pop_back()` function removes the last element from the dynamic array. The `at()` and `operator[]` functions return references to elements in the dynamic array at a given position.

