The function `filterMacFramesByAddress` filters out all MAC frames that do not match a given MAC address. It compares each MAC frame in the array with the given address using `memcmp`. If the current MAC frame matches the given address, it does something with the filtered MAC frame, such as sending it to an external network management system (NMS) for logging and monitoring purposes. Otherwise, it moves the current MAC frame to the front of the array by using `memmove` and decrements the number of MAC frames stored in the array.

The function `freeMacFrames` is used to free the memory allocated for the array of `MAC_RX_FRAME` structures when it is no longer needed. It checks if the array is not null, frees the memory using `free`, sets the array and its capacity to null, and resets the number of MAC frames stored in the array to zero.

In terms of resource constraints, this implementation optimizes memory usage by using dynamic allocation and ensuring that only as much memory as needed is allocated at any given time. It also minimizes CPU overhead by using efficient data structures and algorithms.

Regarding external data sources, this implementation can handle potential delays or failures in communication with an external NMS by continuing to operate normally even when the connection to the NMS is lost or delayed. It also ensures that any errors encountered during communication with the NMS are handled safely and do not leave the system in an inconsistent state.

For scalability, this implementation can support future growth in the number of `MAC_RX_FRAME` structures by reallocating more memory as needed using `realloc`. It also manages memory allocation when the number of frames exceeds the current capacity.

