To handle efficient memory management, this code uses dynamic allocation for the project structures and reallocates the project array as needed to accommodate new projects. It also frees memory when removing projects from the list or after a cleanup operation.

The code includes error handling mechanisms to prevent resource leaks and minimize the impact on other running processes in case of transient errors or failed API calls during synchronization.

To ensure data integrity, this code uses locking mechanisms to serialize access to the project list and ensures that no two threads modify the list simultaneously. It also periodically saves the project list to disk using serialization and deserialization functionalities to persist the data even in case of system failures or power outages.

Finally, this code provides a way to export the list of completed projects to another file for integration with external applications by providing an `export_completed_projects` function that writes the number of completed projects and each project structure to the output file. It also includes an `import_completed_projects` function to import such data from a file and append it to the existing list of projects.']

