In this example, the distributed system uses a combination of mutexes and conditional variables to ensure that only one thread can access the shared data structures at a time, while also allowing other threads to be blocked until they are notified of certain events. The use of mutexes ensures that the shared data structures are consistent across all nodes in the distributed system, while the use of condition variables allows for efficient blocking and waking up of threads waiting on a specific event.

This is just one example of how a distributed system can be implemented using C++11 features. Other approaches may be more appropriate depending on the specific requirements of your application.']

